course: dictionary
lesson: 8314
title: Robbery: crime or rip-off?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Robbery is a noun, a naming word for a crime. It means stealing from a person or place, and the thief uses force or threats to do it. A robbery is more serious than theft, because the thief uses force or fear.
explain: In everyday talk the word can mean something else. «Dad calls it "daylight robbery".» Dad is not saying a crime happened. He is complaining that the price is far too high. The words around it give you the clue.
explain: Always check the context. In a crime story, a bank robbery is a serious crime. When someone says daylight robbery about a shop price, they mean a rip-off.
fact: Robbery means the crime of stealing from a person or place, especially using force or threats.
fact: Robbery is worse than theft because it uses force or fear.
fact: «Bank robbery» and «armed robbery» are serious crimes that often appear in news and crime stories.
fact: «Daylight robbery» is informal: it means a price that is far too expensive.
fact: You can say «a robbery» or «robberies» for single crimes or several crimes, but the word robbery on its own is used for the crime in general.
exercise: «The shop was held up in a robbery at noon, and the thief threatened the cashier.» What is a robbery? | *A crime of stealing with force or threats | A friendly game played in the park | A very cheap sale | A long walk to school
exercise: Dad says «$10 for a drink is daylight robbery!» What does he mean? | The shop was open in daylight | *The drink is far too expensive | A thief took his money at the bank | The drink was free
exercise: Which is the more serious crime? | Theft, because it always uses force | Forgetting to pay a bill | Neither, they are the same | *A robbery, because it uses force or threats
test: What does «bank robbery» suggest? | A cheap loan | A friendly visit to the bank | A game for children | *A serious crime
test: Which phrase means an informal rip-off price? | *daylight robbery | threat | theft | armed robbery
exam: Why does «Dad calls it "daylight robbery".» probably mean a high price and not a crime? | Because it happened at night | Because he saw a thief | *Because he is complaining about the cost, not reporting a crime | Because he is a police officer
